          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A simple class of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoids"} monoids
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categories"} categories
            that permit an effective graphic display via their
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presheaf"} presheaf
            categories.
          %p
            Graphic monoids arise in the study of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/arrangement+of+hyperplanes"} hyperplane arrangements
            and are often called
            %strong left regular band monoids
            in this context.
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            A monoid
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            is called
            %em graphic
            if all
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x,y\in M
            satisfy the so called
            %em graphic identity
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mi y
                  %mi x
                  %mo =
                  %mi x
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x y x=x y
            \. A category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒢
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{G}
            is called
            %em graphic
            if the endomorphism monoid
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi End
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} End(x)
            is a graphic monoid for each object
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
          %h3#remark Remark
          %p
            The terminology originates with Lawvere (
            %a{:href => "#Law89b"}> 1989b
            ) where graphic monoids and categories
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒢
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{G}
            are demanded to be
            = succeed "." do
              %em finite
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#fin_gen"} see below
          %p
            %em Graphic toposes
            are defined then as
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi FinSet
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi 𝒢
                        %mi op
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} FinSet^{\mathcal{G}^{op}}
            resulting in presheaf categories of finite graph-like ‘displays’ that come with a well-behaved lattice of subtoposes permitting the kind of ‘Hegelian analysis’ that were a primary concern for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lawvere"} Lawvere
            in (
            %a{:href => "#Law89b"}> Law89b
            ,
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Law91"} Law91
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Law02"} Law02
          %p
            In particular, the graphic monoid
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Δ
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta_1
            in the
            %a{:href => "#ex"} example
            and the eight-element
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hegelian+taco"} Hegelian taco
            monoid of (Lawvere
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Law89b"} 1989b
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/level"} essential subtoposes
            occuring in Lawvere’s mathematical rendering of basic concepts of Hegel’s dialectic logic.
            %sup#fnref:1
              %a{:href => "#fn:1", :rel => "footnote"} 1
          %p This bears some resemblance to the other context, namely hyperplane arrangements, in which such monoids mainly arise.
          %p
            In the literature that approaches graphic monoids as special class of semigroups, they are called
            %strong unital left regular bands
            or
            %em left regular band monoids
            (cf. Brown (
            %a{:href => "#brown00"}> 2000
            ), Aguiar-Mahajan (
            %a{:href => "#AgMah06"}> 2006
            ), Margolis-Saliola-Steinberg (
            %a{:href => "#MSS15"}> 2015
            )).
          %h2#ex Example
          %p
            The principal example of a graphic category is
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Δ
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta_1
            the three element monoid
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %msub
                    %mi δ
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi δ
                    %mn 2
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{\delta_1,\delta_2,1\}
            with
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi δ
                      %mi i
                    %msub
                      %mi δ
                      %mi j
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi δ
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta_i\delta_j=\delta_i
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Set
                    %mrow
                      %msubsup
                        %mi Δ
                        %mn 1
                        %mi op
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set^{\Delta_1^{op}}
            is the topos of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reflexive+graphs"} reflexive graphs
          %p
            To see this, recall that a
            %em reflexive graph
            consists of a set of vertices
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi s
                    %mo ,
                    %mi t
                    %mo :
                    %mi E
                    %mo →
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s, t : E \to V
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                  %mo :
                  %mi V
                  %mo →
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i : V \to E
            assigning to each vertex an ‘identity edge’ from that vertex to itself, so that
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi s
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi v
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi t
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi v
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi v
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s(i(v)) = t(i(v)) = v
          %p
            We can describe a reflexive graph without ever mentioning its vertices by using the identity edge
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi v
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i(v)
            as a stand-in for
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi v
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi δ
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi δ
                    %mn 2
                  %mo :
                  %mi E
                  %mo →
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta_1, \delta_2 : E \to E
            by
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi δ
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi e
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi s
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi e
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                  %msub
                    %mi δ
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi e
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi e
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta_1(e) = i(s(e)), \qquad \delta_2(e) = i(t(e))
          %p and note that
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi δ
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi δ
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi e
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi δ
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi δ
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi e
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi δ
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi e
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta_1(\delta_1(e)) = \delta_2(\delta_1(e)) = \delta_1(e)
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi δ
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi δ
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi e
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi δ
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi δ
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi e
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi δ
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi e
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta_1(\delta_2(e)) = \delta_2(\delta_2(e)) = \delta_2(e)
          %p
            Thus, the reflexive graph determines a functor
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo :
                    %msubsup
                      %mi Δ
                      %mn 1
                      %mi op
                    %mo →
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F : \Delta_1^{op} \to \Set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Δ
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta_1
            as a one-object category. In other words, it gives a presheaf on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Δ
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta_1
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Δ
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta_1
            determines a reflexive graph.
          %p
            The more familiar two-sorted ‘signature’ for reflexive graphs can be obtained from the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cauchy+completion"} Cauchy completion
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mover
                      %mi Δ
                      %mo ¯
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \overline\Delta_1
            by identifying the isomorphic descendents of
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi δ
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi δ
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta_1,\delta_2
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mover
                      %mi Δ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tilde\Delta_1
            has two objects and is basically an augmentation with reflexivity data of the familiar diagram
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                  %mo ⇉
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V\rightrightarrows E
            that underlies the topos of (“irreflexive”) directed graphs, or
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quiver"} quivers
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Δ
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mover
                      %mi Δ
                      %mo ¯
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mover
                      %mi Δ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta_1,\overline\Delta_1,\tilde\Delta_1
            are graphic categories and the resulting presheaf toposes are, of course, equivalent.
          %h2#properties Properties
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Lawvere calls the graphic identity
                = succeed "\u2019" do
                  %em ‘the least common generalization of constant (x=c) and identity (x=1)
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "#Law89b"} 1989b
                %em constant
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi c
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
                is meant here a
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi c
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi M
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c\in M
                such that
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi c
                      %mi y
                      %mo =
                      %mi c
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c y=c
                for all
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi y
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi M
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y\in M
                = succeed "\u2019" do
                  %em chambers
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a{:href => "#AgMah06"} Aguiar-Mahajan 2006
            %li
              %p
                In a graphic monoid every element is
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/idempotent"} idempotent
                as can be directly seen from the graphic identity with
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi y
                        %mo =
                        %mn 1
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y=1
            %li
              %p
                It follows that a commutative graphic monoid is the same as a
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semilattice"} semilattice
            %li
              %p
                More generally, one can define a partial order on
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi M
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
                by
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ≤
                      %mi y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\leq y
                iff
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mi y
                      %mo =
                      %mi y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x y=y
                (‘
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
                is a face of
                = succeed "\u2019)." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi y
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
                %sup#fnref:2
                  %a{:href => "#fn:2", :rel => "footnote"} 2
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                The
                %em free graphic monoid
                on a set
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                is the set of totally ordered finite subsets of
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi S
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi T
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
                is the union
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi S
                      %mo ∪
                      %mi T
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S \cup T
                ordered in such a way that the inclusion
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi S
                      %mo ↪
                      %mi S
                      %mo ∪
                      %mi T
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S \hookrightarrow S\cup T
                is order-preserving, the inclusion
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi T
                      %mo ↪
                      %mi S
                      %mo ∪
                      %mi T
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T \hookrightarrow S\cup T
                is order-preserving for all elements of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi T
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
                not in
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi S
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi T
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
                not in
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi S
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
                are greater than all elements of
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi S
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                        %mo =
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                        %mi a
                        %mo ,
                        %mi b
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X = \{a,b\}
              %p
                1 (corresponding to the empty subset of
                = succeed ")," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
                (corresponding to the subset
                = succeed ")," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                        %mi a
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{a\}
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi b
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
                (corresponding to
                = succeed ")," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                        %mi b
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{b\}
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                      %mi b
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a b
                (corresponding to
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mi a
                      %mo ,
                      %mi b
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{a,b\}
                ordered so that
                = succeed ")," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi a
                        %mo &lt;
                        %mi b
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \lt b
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi b
                      %mi a
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b a
                (corresponding to
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mi a
                      %mo ,
                      %mi b
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{a,b\}
                ordered so that
                = succeed ")," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi b
                        %mo &lt;
                        %mi a
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b \lt a
              %p
                and nothing else, since for example
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                      %mi b
                      %mi b
                      %mo =
                      %mi a
                      %mi b
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a b b = a b
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi b
                      %mi a
                      %mi b
                      %mo =
                      %mi b
                      %mi a
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b a b = b a
                (since the union of ordered subsets
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mi a
                      %mo ,
                      %mi b
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %mo ∪
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mi b
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mi a
                      %mo ,
                      %mi b
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{a,b\} \cup \{b\} = \{a, b\}
                inherits the same ordering that
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mi a
                      %mo ,
                      %mi b
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{a,b\}
                had).
            %li#fin_gen
              %p
                In particular, for a finite set of generators the free graphic monoid is finite and consists of all words without repetitions of which there are exactly
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo !
                      %msubsup
                        %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                        %mrow
                          %mi i
                          %mo =
                          %mn 0
                        %mi n
                      %mfrac
                        %mn 1
                        %mrow
                          %mi i
                          %mo !
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n!\sum_{i=0}^n \frac{1}{i!}
                over an alphabet with
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
                letters.
                %sup#fnref:3
                  %a{:href => "#fn:3", :rel => "footnote"} 3
          %ul
            %li
              The variety (in the sense of universal algebra) of graphic monoids is generated by
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Δ
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta_1
              (cf. Lawvere (
              %a{:href => "#Law91"}> 1991
              )).
          %h3#graphic_monoids_as_shelves Graphic monoids as shelves
          %p
            There is a relation between graphic monoids and
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/shelf"} shelves
          #GraphicsAsShelves.num_prop
            %h6#theorem Theorem
            %p
              A graphic monoid is the same as a
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  unital
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+shelf"} left shelf
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi b
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a (b c) = (a b)(a c)
            %p
              and has an element
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
              serving as a left and right unit:
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                    %mi a
                    %mo =
                    %mi a
                    %mo =
                    %mi a
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1 a = a = a 1
          .proof
            %h6#proof Proof
            %p First start with a unital left shelf. Note that the graphic identity holds:
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mi b
                    %mo =
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi b
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a  b = a (b 1) = (a b) (a 1) = (a  b)  a
            %p and also
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mi b
                    %mo =
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a  b = a  (1  b) = (a  1)  (a  b) = a  (a  b)
            %p Using these and self-distributivity we can deduce the associative law as follows:
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable{:columnalign => "right left right left right left right left right left", :columnspacing => "0em", :displaystyle => "true"}
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi a
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi b
                            %mi c
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mo =
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi a
                            %mi b
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi a
                            %mi c
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo =
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi a
                            %mi b
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mi a
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi a
                            %mi b
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mi c
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo =
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi a
                            %mi b
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi a
                            %mi b
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mi c
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo =
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi a
                            %mi b
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mi c
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\begin{aligned}
                    a  (b  c) &amp;= (a  b)  (a  c) \\
                    \&amp;= ((a  b)  a)  ((a  b)  c) \\
                    \&amp;= (a  b)  ((a  b)  c) \\
                    \&amp;= (a  b)  c
                    \\end{aligned}
            %p So, we have a graphic monoid.
            %p Conversely, suppose we start with a graphic monoid. Then we can prove the left self-distributive law as follows:
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi b
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi c
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mi b
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi c
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a (b c) = (a b) c = (a b a) c = (a b)(a c)
          %p
            Note in particular that, somewhat surprisingly, the
            %strong associativity
            in a unital left shelf comes for free! For more background see the comments on:
          %ul
            %li
              %a{:href => "https://golem.ph.utexas.edu/category/2015/05/the_origin_of_the_word_quandle.html#c049149"}> The origin of the word “quandle”
              ,
              = succeed "-Category" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          %h2#related_pages Related Pages
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %span.newWikiWord
                  graphic topos
                  %a{:href => "/nlab/new/graphic+topos"} ?
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hegelian+taco"} Hegelian taco
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/shelf"} shelf
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/band"} band
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rectangular+band"} rectangular band
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Aufhebung"} Aufhebung
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/graph"} graph
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/band"} band
          %h2#references References
          %ul
            %li#AgMah06
              %p
                M. Aguiar, S. Mahajan,
                %em Coxeter Groups and Hopf Algebras
                , AMS Providence 2006. (
                %a{:href => "http://www.math.cornell.edu/~maguiar/monograph.pdf"}> draft
                )
            %li#brown00
              %p
                K. S. Brown,
                %em Semigroups, Semirings, and Markov Chains
                , J. Theor. Prob.
                %strong 13
                no.3 (2000) pp.871-938.
            %li
              %p
                R. Guitart,
                %em Autocategories: II. Autographic algebras
                , Cah. Top. Géom. Diff. Cat.
                %strong LV
                (2014) pp.151-160.
            %li
              %p
                N. Kimura,
                %em The structure of idempotent semigroups I
                , Pacific Journal of Mathematics
                %strong 8
                no.2 (1958) pp.257-275. (
                %a{:href => "http://msp.org/pjm/1958/8-2/pjm-v8-n2-p07-p.pdf"}> pdf
                )
            %li
              %p
                F. Klein-Barmen,
                %em Über eine weitere Verallgemeinerung des Verbandsbegriffs
                , Math. Z.
                %strong 46
                (1940) pp.472-480.(
                %a{:href => "http://gdz.sub.uni-goettingen.de/dms/load/img/?PPN=GDZPPN00237921X"}> gdz
                )
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/F.+W.+Lawvere"}> F. W. Lawvere
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Qualitative distinctions between some toposes of generalized graphs
                %strong 92
                (1989) pp. 261-299.
            %li#Law89b
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/F.+W.+Lawvere"}> F. W. Lawvere
                ,
                %em Display of graphics and their applications, as exemplified by 2-categories and the Hegelian “taco”
                Proceedings of the first international conference on algebraic methodology and software technology University of Iowa, May 22-24 1989, Iowa City, pp. 51-74.
            %li#Law91
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/F.+W.+Lawvere"}> F. W. Lawvere
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em More on graphic toposes
                %strong XXXII
                no. 1 (1991) pp.5-10. (
                %a{:href => "http://archive.numdam.org/article/CTGDC_1991__32_1_5_0.pdf"}> pdf
                )
            %li#Law02
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/F.+W.+Lawvere"}> F. W. Lawvere
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Linearization of graphic toposes via Coxeter groups
                %strong 168
                (2002) pp. 425-436. (
                %a{:href => "http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0022404901001074/pdfft?md5=a4ca9bc67df6ae63ddf53c559bd71315&pid=1-s2.0-S0022404901001074-main.pdf"}> pdf
                )
            %li#MSS15
              %p
                S. Margolis, F. Saliola, B. Steinberg,
                %em Cell Complexes, Poset Topology and the Representation Theory of Algebras Arising in Algebraic Combinatorics and Discrete Geometry
                , arXiv:1508.05446 (2015). (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1508.05446v1"}> abstract
                )
            %li
              %p
                M.-P. Schützenberger,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Sur certains treillis gauches
                %strong 225
                (1947) pp.277–278. (
                %a{:href => "http://igm.univ-mlv.fr/~berstel/Mps/Travaux/A/1947TreillisGauchesCras.pdf"}> pdf
                )
          .footnotes
            %hr/
            %ol
              %li#fn:1
                %p
                  For more on Lawvere’s approach to Hegelian dialectics see
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Aufhebung"} Aufhebung
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+cylinder"} adjoint cylinder
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Science+of+Logic"} Science of Logic
                  %a{:href => "#fnref:1", :rev => "footnote"} ↩
              %li#fn:2
                %p
                  Semigroup theorists typically order a graphic monoid by
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %mo ≤
                        %mi y
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\leq y
                  if
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi y
                        %mi x
                        %mo =
                        %mi x
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y x=x
                  because this is equivalent to
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi x
                          %mi M
                          %mo ⊆
                          %mi y
                          %mi M
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x M\subseteq y M
                  %a{:href => "#fnref:2", :rev => "footnote"} ↩
              %li#fn:3
                %p
                  The finiteness was basically already observed in Schützenberger (
                  %a{:href => "#Sch&#252;tz47"}> 1947
                  ). See also Lawvere (
                  %a{:href => "#Law89b"}> 1989b
                  ).
                  %a{:href => "#fnref:3", :rev => "footnote"} ↩
